---
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css"
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css"
import MarketingLayout from "../components/marketing/MarketingLayout.astro"

export const prerender = true

// Comparison grid. Each cell is [mark, text]: y = yes, p = partly, n = no. Facts come from each company's own docs and
// pricing pages, re-checked 15 October 2026 (see the sources list at the foot of the page). Cursor moved to new plans
// on 12 October 2026, so its cells follow the new pricing page.
const products = [
  { name: "Cursor", kind: "AI code editor (SpaceX)" },
  { name: "Codex", kind: "OpenAI" },
  { name: "Claude Code", kind: "Anthropic" },
  { name: "Lovable", kind: "App builder" },
  { name: "Base44", kind: "App builder (Wix)" },
]

const groups = [
  {
    name: "Agents",
    rows: [
      {
        cap: "Parallel agents, isolated",
        note: "Each task in its own git worktree",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Agents Window, worktrees, cloud agents"],
          ["y", "Worktrees in the app, cloud tasks"],
          ["y", "Worktrees, /batch, cloud sessions"],
          ["p", "Drafts: a separate copy and chat per person"],
          ["n", "None documented"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Clash flags, then merge after your tests and a secret scan"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Runs other vendors' agents",
        // Conductor and Superset are not columns in this grid, so the row itself says they do this too.
        note: "Side by side, in one place; orchestrators such as Conductor and Superset do this too",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Their editor extensions, in separate panels"],
          ["n", "Imports their settings, runs its own"],
          ["n", "Workers are always Claude sessions"],
          ["n", "Its own agent only"],
          ["n", "Its own agent only"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Claude Code, Codex and Cursor Agent, each in an isolated checkout"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Specialist subagents",
        note: "Delegated work that reports back",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Custom subagents"],
          ["y", "Custom agents; worker and explorer built in"],
          ["y", "Custom subagents; agent teams (experimental)"],
          ["p", "Read-only research and review helpers"],
          ["n", "None documented"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "8 built in: explore, review, judge, security, debug, test, performance, migration"],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Quality",
    rows: [
      {
        cap: "AI review on pull requests",
        note: "In place of a separate review bot",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Bugbot from the $30 plan, about $1–1.50 a run"],
          ["y", "@codex review, on plans from $20/mo"],
          ["y", "Team and Enterprise, billed on tokens; Anthropic puts the average at $15–25 a review"],
          ["n", "Security scan before publishing"],
          ["n", "App security scan"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Vectorscope on GitHub, desktop and CLI; no Vector fee, only model tokens"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Issue to pull request",
        note: "From a ticket to a reviewable branch",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Linear, GitHub or Slack to a cloud-agent PR"],
          ["p", "Linear issue to a cloud task; you open the PR"],
          ["y", "@claude in its GitHub Action"],
          ["n", "None"],
          ["n", "None"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "An issue comment becomes a branch and a PR listing the commands it ran and their results"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Browser testing",
        note: "Of the running app",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Browser tool with console, network, screenshots"],
          ["p", "In the desktop app only"],
          ["p", "Chrome extension, direct Anthropic plans only"],
          ["y", "Remote browser against the preview"],
          ["p", "Testing agent; cannot test sign-in steps"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Browser agent; Vercel deploys get a browser smoke test by default"],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Ship and collaborate",
    rows: [
      {
        cap: "Deploy and database from the agent",
        note: "Publish, migrate, set environment",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Through Vercel and Supabase plugins"],
          ["p", "Through third-party plugins"],
          ["p", "Through third-party plugins"],
          ["y", "Built-in hosting and database"],
          ["y", "Built-in backend, database and hosting"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Vercel publishing with health checks; Supabase migrations with a dry run"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Live multiplayer",
        note: "Teammates in the same live work",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Read-only shared transcripts (Teams)"],
          ["n", "None documented"],
          ["p", "Shared session links, not live"],
          ["y", "Collaborators see changes as they happen"],
          ["y", "Real-time canvas with named cursors"],
        ],
        vector: ["p", "Early: a teammate joins a live session by invite link"],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Cost and access",
    rows: [
      {
        cap: "Cost of every session",
        note: "In dollars, per session and model",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Monthly spend limits; no per-task cost documented"],
          ["p", "Workspace spend controls; no per-task cost documented"],
          ["p", "Session estimate and limits"],
          ["p", "Credits per message, credit limits"],
          ["p", "Monthly credits, not dollars"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Sessions priced from provider-reported tokens wherever the model has a price, subagents included, with model advice"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Any model, any provider",
        note: "Including local and free models",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Hosted models; own keys for 5 providers"],
          ["p", "OpenAI models by default; Responses-compatible providers, Bedrock, Ollama or LM Studio"],
          ["p", "Officially Claude models only; others through a gateway, without Anthropic support"],
          ["n", "No model picker documented"],
          ["p", "10 models; picking one needs a paid plan"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "219 providers, local endpoints, free models"],
      },
      {
        cap: "Your repos, on your machine",
        note: "Any existing codebase, run locally",
        cells: [
          ["y", "Any local or remote repo"],
          ["y", "Local repos and cloud tasks"],
          ["y", "Any repo, terminal and IDE"],
          ["p", "Cloud projects; cannot import a repo"],
          ["p", "Imports a repo into its cloud"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Desktop and CLI on your machine"],
      },
      {
        cap: "The whole product, free",
        note: "Agents, review and testing included",
        cells: [
          ["p", "Limited free tier; review and cloud agents from $30/mo"],
          ["p", "Free desktop tier; CLI, IDE and review from $20/mo"],
          ["n", "Not on the free plan; from $17/mo"],
          ["p", "5 build credits a day free, 30 a month"],
          ["p", "25 messages a month free"],
        ],
        vector: ["y", "Free workspace; bring a model account or use free models"],
      },
    ],
  },
]

const allRows = groups.flatMap((group) => group.rows)
const fullMarks = products.map((_, index) => allRows.filter((row) => row.cells[index][0] === "y").length)
const vectorFullMarks = allRows.filter((row) => row.vector[0] === "y").length
const closest = products[fullMarks.indexOf(Math.max(...fullMarks))]
const markLabel = { y: "Yes", p: "Partly", n: "No" }
const markClass = { y: "ip-yes", p: "ip-part", n: "ip-no" }
const vectorMarkClass = { y: "ip-vec-yes", p: "ip-vec-part", n: "ip-no" }

// Open-weight, coding-specialised models since 2024 by total parameters (billions), from their public model cards.
// x is the position on a log scale from 5B to 1.5T; y is a beeswarm offset in pixels so close sizes do not overlap.
const scale = (billions: number) => ((Math.log10(billions) - Math.log10(5)) / (Math.log10(1500) - Math.log10(5))) * 100
const models = [
  { name: "Seed-Coder-8B", maker: "ByteDance Seed", size: 8, y: 0 },
  { name: "Mellum2", maker: "JetBrains", size: 12, y: 0 },
  { name: "DeepSeek-Coder-V2-Lite", maker: "DeepSeek", size: 16, y: 0 },
  { name: "Codestral", maker: "Mistral", size: 22, y: 0 },
  { name: "Devstral Small 2", maker: "Mistral", size: 24, y: 12 },
  { name: "North Mini Code", maker: "Cohere", size: 30, y: -12 },
  { name: "Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B", maker: "Qwen", size: 30.5, y: 12 },
  { name: "Qwen2.5-Coder-32B", maker: "Qwen", size: 32, y: -24 },
  { name: "KAT-Dev", maker: "Kwaipilot", size: 33, y: 24 },
  { name: "Laguna XS 2.1", maker: "Poolside", size: 33, y: -36 },
  { name: "KAT-Coder-V2.5-Dev", maker: "Kwaipilot", size: 35, y: 0 },
  { name: "Kimi-Dev-72B", maker: "Moonshot", size: 72, y: 0 },
  { name: "KAT-Dev-72B-Exp", maker: "Kwaipilot", size: 72, y: -12 },
  { name: "Qwen3-Coder-Next", maker: "Qwen", size: 80, y: 12 },
  { name: "Laguna S 2.1", maker: "Poolside", size: 118, y: 0 },
  { name: "Devstral 2", maker: "Mistral", size: 123, y: -12 },
  { name: "Laguna M.1", maker: "Poolside", size: 225, y: 0 },
  { name: "DeepSeek-Coder-V2", maker: "DeepSeek", size: 236, y: -12 },
  { name: "Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B", maker: "Qwen", size: 480, y: 0 },
  { name: "Kimi K2.7 Code", maker: "Moonshot", size: 1000, y: 0 },
]
const sizeLabel = (billions: number) => (billions >= 1000 ? `${billions / 1000}T` : `${billions}B`)
const buckets = [
  { label: "8–35B", models: models.filter((model) => model.size <= 35) },
  { label: "72–123B", models: models.filter((model) => model.size > 35 && model.size < 200) },
  { label: "225B–1T", models: models.filter((model) => model.size >= 200) },
]
const bucketCentre = (bucket: (typeof buckets)[number]) =>
  (scale(bucket.models[0].size) + scale(bucket.models[bucket.models.length - 1].size)) / 2
const ticks = [10, 30, 100, 300, 1000]
// Velocity's planned size is "over 300 billion", as the README and the landing page have said since September 2026.
const velocityAt = scale(300)

const stack = [
  {
    job: "AI code review",
    today: "CodeRabbit $24–30 per developer · Greptile $30 per seat plus credits · Graphite $40 per user · Cursor Bugbot about $1–1.50 a run · Claude Code Review billed on tokens, averaging $15–25 a review",
    vector: "Vectorscope reviews pull requests on GitHub, in the desktop app and in the CLI, with a security reviewer on sensitive paths, a verify pass that re-checks blocking findings and drops the ones it cannot confirm, and a spend limit per review.",
    fit: "Replaces on GitHub",
  },
  {
    job: "Multi-agent orchestration",
    today: "Conductor free on Mac, $50 Pro, $60 per user Teams · Superset free for one user, $15–20 per user",
    vector: "Vector's agent and your installed Claude Code, Codex and Cursor Agent run in parallel, isolated checkouts on one dashboard, merged after your tests and a secret scan.",
    fit: "Replaces for local work",
  },
  {
    job: "Agent cost tracking",
    today: "Helicone $79 Pro · Langfuse $29 Core, $199 Pro",
    vector: "Tokenomics prices Vector sessions from provider-reported tokens wherever the model has a price, subagents included, labels free, ChatGPT plan, Codium and unpriced steps instead, and recommends models from measured runs.",
    fit: "Covers coding agents",
  },
  {
    job: "Browser testing by an agent",
    today: "Momentic $125 a month plus usage · QA Wolf billed per credit and runner minute",
    vector: "A browser agent tests the running app, and Vercel deploys get a health check and a browser smoke test by default.",
    fit: "Overlaps",
  },
  {
    job: "Separate agent apps",
    today: "Without an orchestrator, Claude Code, Codex and Cursor each run in their own window with their own history",
    vector: "One dashboard runs all of them on your existing subscriptions, next to Vector's own agent.",
    fit: "Runs them",
  },
  {
    job: "Deploy and database consoles",
    today: "Vercel and Supabase dashboards, opened by hand",
    vector: "Publishing, environment variables and Supabase migrations with a dry run, started from the task.",
    fit: "Controls them",
  },
]
---

{/* The investor one-pager. Unlisted: noindex, not linked from the site, shared by link only. */}
<MarketingLayout
  title="Vector · Investor one-pager"
  description="Vector, the control room for AI coding agents: how it compares, the tools it replaces, why it plans to build its own coding model, the offline cost benchmark, roadmap and business model."
  noindex
>
  <div class="ip-root">
<div class="ip-page">
  <header class="ip-top">
    <div class="ip-brand">
      <span class="ip-mark" aria-hidden="true"><svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14"><path d="M1.5 2.5 L7 12 L12.5 2.5" fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></span>
      Vector
    </div>
    <div class="ip-meta"><span>vectordev.ai</span><span>Agentic coding workspace</span><span>October 2026</span></div>
  </header>

  <section class="ip-thesis" aria-labelledby="thesis-title">
    <div class="ip-eyebrow">For teams that build with AI agents</div>
    <h1 id="thesis-title">The control room for AI <em>coding agents</em>.</h1>
    <p class="ip-lede">Teams now use several coding agents, such as Claude Code, Codex and Cursor, then pay for a review bot, an orchestration app and a cost tracker on top, often per seat. Vector puts that stack in one free workspace: its own agent beside Claude Code, Codex and Cursor Agent, any model, code review, browser testing, deploys and a price on each Vector session whose model has one. Next, it plans to build <strong>Vector Velocity</strong>, its own large coding model for that loop.</p>
    <div class="ip-facts">
      <div class="ip-fact"><strong class="ip-num">219</strong><span>providers in the model catalog, plus local and free models</span></div>
      <div class="ip-fact"><strong class="ip-num">3</strong><span>outside agents run inside Vector: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor Agent</span></div>
      <div class="ip-fact"><strong class="ip-num">58–64%</strong><span>lower modelled request cost than Claude Code on the same scripted tasks, offline*</span></div>
      <div class="ip-fact"><strong class="ip-num">$0</strong><span>for the workspace on macOS, Windows, Linux and the terminal</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section ip-two" aria-label="Problem and product">
    <div class="ip-problem">
      <div class="ip-section-head">
        <div class="ip-eyebrow">The problem</div>
        <h2>AI agents multiplied. Control didn't.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="ip-clean">
        <li><strong>Without an orchestrator, every agent is a separate island.</strong> Each vendor's agent runs in its own window, so three agents means three places to look.</li>
        <li><strong>The labs' own agents are built around their own models:</strong> Claude Code officially supports only Claude models, and Codex defaults to OpenAI's, with other providers through compatible endpoints, though 27% of organisations already send different work to different models.<sup>1</sup></li>
        <li><strong>The rest is a stack of subscriptions.</strong> AI review, agent orchestration and cost tracking are bought separately, review and orchestration often per developer.</li>
        <li><strong>The bill per task is hard to see.</strong> Spend mostly arrives as credits, limits or a monthly invoice, and no single tool prices each task across vendors, while 38% of organisations already set usage limits to control AI cost.<sup>1</sup></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-section ip-today">
      <div class="ip-section-head">
        <div class="ip-eyebrow">What Vector does today</div>
        <h2>One workspace. Your agents. Any model.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-caps">
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Your agents, one dashboard</h3><p>Vector's agent and your installed Claude Code, Codex and Cursor Agent each run in an isolated checkout, on one dashboard that flags clashes, then merge back after your tests and a secret scan.</p></div>
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Any model</h3><p>8,184 models from 219 providers in Vector's catalog, connected with your own key or sign-in, plus any OpenAI-compatible or local endpoint and free models through OpenRouter.</p></div>
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Vectorscope review</h3><p>Pull-request review on GitHub, desktop and CLI, with a verify pass that re-checks blocking findings and drops the ones it cannot confirm. An issue comment becomes a branch and a PR listing the commands it ran and their results.</p></div>
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Specialist subagents</h3><p>Explore, Review, Judge, Security, Debug, Test, Performance and Migration specialists run in the background and report back.</p></div>
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Tokenomics</h3><p>Vector prices each session from the tokens the provider reported wherever the model has a price, subagents included. Free models, ChatGPT plan, Codium and unpriced steps are labelled instead of priced. Vector recommends models once a task type has three measured runs.</p></div>
        <div class="ip-cap"><h3>Test and ship from the task</h3><p>A browser agent tests the running app. Environment variables, Supabase migrations with a dry run, and Vercel publishing with health and browser checks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>New in desktop 1.99.108, published 17 October 2026:</strong> fuller Vercel and Supabase controls, with approval for every account change and the exact SQL shown for database changes; Models included with Vector under a no-training policy, through your own free OpenRouter account or, while Vector's service has it on, Vector's shared allowance for a free Vector account; Codium plans, with sales marked Coming soon until payments are connected; and helper sessions such as reviews kept out of the session list while their tokens and cost stay visible.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Where Vector is different</div>
      <h2 id="compare-title">Twelve capabilities, six products</h2>
      <p>Each cell says how that product handles it today. Vector chose these rows because they are the jobs it is built for: it has {vectorFullMarks} of the {allRows.length} in full, and the closest in this grid, {closest.name}, has {Math.max(...fullMarks)}.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-legend" aria-label="Legend">
      <span><i class="ip-sym ip-yes" aria-hidden="true">✓</i>Yes</span>
      <span><i class="ip-sym ip-part" aria-hidden="true"></i>Partly</span>
      <span><i class="ip-sym ip-no" aria-hidden="true">✕</i>No</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-grid-wrap">
      <table class="ip-compare">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Capability</th>
            {products.map((product) => <th scope="col">{product.name}<small>{product.kind}</small></th>)}
            <th scope="col" class="ip-vec">Vector<small>Agent workspace</small></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        {groups.map((group) => (
          <tbody>
            <tr class="ip-group"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="7">{group.name}</th></tr>
            {group.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.cap}<span>{row.note}</span></th>
                {row.cells.map((cell, index) => (
                  <td data-label={products[index].name}>
                    <i class={`ip-sym ${markClass[cell[0] as "y" | "p" | "n"]}`} aria-label={markLabel[cell[0] as "y" | "p" | "n"]}>{cell[0] === "y" ? "✓" : cell[0] === "n" ? "✕" : ""}</i>
                    <span class="ip-cell">{cell[1]}</span>
                  </td>
                ))}
                <td class="ip-vec" data-label="Vector">
                  <i class={`ip-sym ${vectorMarkClass[row.vector[0] as "y" | "p" | "n"]}`} aria-label={markLabel[row.vector[0] as "y" | "p" | "n"]}>{row.vector[0] === "y" ? "✓" : ""}</i>
                  <span class="ip-cell">{row.vector[1]}</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        ))}
        <tfoot>
          <tr class="ip-tally">
            <th scope="row">Full marks<span>Rows marked Yes, out of {allRows.length}</span></th>
            {fullMarks.map((count, index) => <td data-label={products[index].name}><b class="ip-num">{count}</b></td>)}
            <td class="ip-vec" data-label="Vector"><b class="ip-num">{vectorFullMarks}</b></td>
          </tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>Where Vector matches the field:</strong> parallel agents in worktrees, subagents, AI pull-request review, issue-to-PR, browser testing and local repos. Cursor, Codex and Claude Code have these too, and Lovable and Base44 lead on live collaboration and built-in hosting. Orchestrators such as Conductor and Superset, not in this grid, also run Claude Code, Codex and other agents side by side in isolated workspaces. Vector's edge is running them beside its own agent, on any model, with a price on each Vector session whose model has one. Competitor details come from each company's official docs and pricing pages, checked 15 October 2026; sources are listed at the end.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="stack-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">What Vector consolidates</div>
      <h2 id="stack-title">One workspace instead of a stack of tools</h2>
      <p>Teams buy each of these jobs separately today, often per developer. Vector does the core job of each inside the free workspace, on the team's own model account. The specialists go deeper in their own lanes; Vector's advantage is that the jobs share one place, one set of agents and one bill.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-stack" role="list">
      <div class="ip-stack-head" aria-hidden="true"><span>Job</span><span>What teams buy today · list price a month</span><span>In Vector, free</span></div>
      {stack.map((item) => (
        <div class="ip-stack-row" role="listitem">
          <h3>{item.job}</h3>
          <p class="ip-stack-today"><span class="ip-stack-tag">Today</span>{item.today}</p>
          <p class="ip-stack-vector"><span class="ip-stack-tag">In Vector</span>{item.vector} <span class="ip-fit">{item.fit}</span></p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="ip-callouts">
      <div class="ip-callout">
        <strong class="ip-num">$240–480<small> a month</small></strong>
        <p>What a 10-developer team pays CodeRabbit for AI review alone at list price, from Essentials ($24 a developer) to Team ($48), billed annually, before any model usage. In Vector, Vectorscope does that job on GitHub inside the free workspace; reviews run on the team's own model account, so they still use tokens, capped by default at $2 a review.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-callout">
        <strong class="ip-num">$1.5B</strong>
        <p>CodeRabbit's valuation for AI review alone (Series C, August 2026), and Cursor agreed to buy Graphite in December 2025. Orchestration on its own is harder to charge for: Vibe Kanban's maker shut down in April 2026 with most users on its free tier. Vector gives the whole workspace away and plans to earn from Codium, the model credits it will sell inside the workspace once payments are connected.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>Controlled, not hosted:</strong> Vector deploys to Vercel and migrates Supabase on the team's own accounts. Hosting, databases and billing for them stay with those providers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Privacy</div>
      <h2 id="privacy-title">Your code stays on your computer, and Vector trains on none of it</h2>
      <p>Vector is local-first. Sessions, history and checkpoints stay on the developer's computer. Vector's servers keep no prompts or code, apart from what a user chooses to publish or send to support. Nothing in Vector collects data for training. Prompts still go to the model provider the user picks, under that provider's rules, so picking the provider is how users control their privacy.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="ip-clean">
      <li><strong>Local first.</strong> Sessions, history and checkpoints are kept on the user's computer, in a local database and Vector's own data folder. Session history does not sync to Vector. The one exception is a share link the user chooses to publish, which is public for 30 days at most.</li>
      <li><strong>No prompts kept on Vector's servers.</strong> With the user's own key or a ChatGPT sign-in, prompts go straight from the computer to that provider. A model on the user's own computer keeps them there. On Vector's own model routes, the gateway passes prompts and replies through without storing or logging them.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing collected for training.</strong> No part of Vector gathers prompts or code to train a model, so there is no setting to switch off. Velocity, the model Vector plans to build, will not be trained on customers' code.</li>
      <li><strong>Zero retention on Codium.</strong> Every Codium request asks OpenRouter to route only to providers it records as zero data retention and to deny data collection. It sends OpenRouter a one-way hash of the account ID, never the user's email or name. <span class="ip-status">In development</span></li>
      <li><strong>No-training free models.</strong> From 1.99.108, every request to an included free model asks that prompts are not used for training, and only providers on Vector's allowlist serve them. These providers are not zero retention and may keep prompts under their own policies.</li>
      <li><strong>Counts, not content.</strong> Usage counts are totals, such as sessions and tokens, with the app version, platform and the models used most. They never include prompts, code, file names, model output or keys, and users can switch them off. Crash reports are off by default, OpenTelemetry sends data only to an endpoint the user sets, and desktop dictation runs on a local Whisper model.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ip-muted"><strong class="ip-ink">How that compares on personal plans.</strong> On personal plans of Codex and Claude Code, whether your sessions train their models depends on a setting. OpenAI may train on ChatGPT and Codex content from Free, Plus and Pro accounts unless the user turns off “Improve the model for everyone”. Codex has a separate “Include environments” setting, and rating a reply can let OpenAI train on that whole conversation even after opting out. Anthropic trains on Claude Free, Pro and Max accounts, Claude Code included, when the user's training setting is on, and keeps that data for up to five years, or 30 days with the setting off. Both companies offer an opt-out, and neither trains on business or API data by default. Vector itself trains on nothing, so it has no such setting. Prompts sent through a ChatGPT sign-in or through Claude Code inside Vector still follow that company's setting.<sup>2</sup></p>
    <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>What Vector cannot change:</strong> a prompt sent to a model provider follows that provider's rules. A user who signs in with ChatGPT, or runs Claude Code or Codex inside Vector, is still under OpenAI's or Anthropic's terms for that account. Codium's zero-retention routing relies on OpenRouter's records and the providers' published policies, not an independent audit. Usage counts are on by default.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">The model layer</div>
      <h2 id="model-title">Why Vector plans to build its own coding model</h2>
      <p>Every coding tool sells software but pays for tokens. Whoever owns the model controls the cost line, the speed and the roadmap.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-reasons">
      <div class="ip-reason">
        <span class="ip-reason-n ip-num">01</span>
        <h3>Model spend is the cost line</h3>
        <p>Coding tools pay for every token they serve. Cursor ran at negative gross margins until its own Composer model and cheaper open models brought it to slightly positive (TechCrunch, April 2026). A model Vector owns lets Vector set its own price and serving speed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-reason">
        <span class="ip-reason-n ip-num">02</span>
        <h3>The model vendors sell competing agents</h3>
        <p>Claude Code alone passed $2.5B in run-rate revenue (Anthropic, February 2026). Access also gets withdrawn: Anthropic cut Windsurf's direct Claude capacity with less than five days' notice (June 2025) and blocked third-party coding tools from using Claude Pro and Max subscriptions (January 2026). After SpaceX bought Cursor, OpenAI said Cursor's access to its models ends on 12 November 2026.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-reason">
        <span class="ip-reason-n ip-num">03</span>
        <h3>Models are trained for one harness</h3>
        <p>OpenAI's guide for its Codex models says they were trained on its own patch format and terminal tool, and may not be on other tools. Cursor trained Composer with reinforcement learning inside Cursor's own agent, and Cognition built SWE-1.5 together with its own harness. On Terminal-Bench 4.0, the top 19 of 35 entries each run a lab's model in that lab's own agent. Vector's loop of specialists, review pass and cross-agent merge checks is nobody's training target but Vector's.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ip-gap">
      <div class="ip-gap-head">
        <h3>Large open coding models are rare, and the two largest are paired with their makers' own agents.</h3>
      </div>
