  <section class="ip-section" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
    <div class="ip-section-head">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Roadmap · from October 2026</div>
      <h2 id="roadmap-title">From running agents to building the model behind them</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-road">
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">Early prototype</span>
        <h3>Computer Use</h3>
        <p>Agents that see and operate desktop apps, not just code and the browser. Vector's own early native helper, with per-app permission grants and input controls, is not in any release yet; a community Computer Use plugin is in the plugin catalog today.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">Early version live</span>
        <h3>Multiplayer</h3>
        <p>Today a teammate can join a live workspace with one invite link. Built but not yet in a published CLI: guests stay inside the workspace, while terminals, shell commands and approvals stay with the host; vector invite runs from the CLI, so this arrives with the next CLI release. Next: Vector Teams with shared defaults, already in the code and waiting on service setup.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">In development</span>
        <h3>Vector Codium</h3>
        <p>Managed coding models on one Vector account, with no provider keys: monthly plans or top-ups. The plans are built into desktop 1.99.108; sales open once payments are connected.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone">
        <span class="ip-stage">Planned</span>
        <h3>Vector Velocity</h3>
        <p>Vector's own coding model, planned at 300B+ parameters, designed for the loop Vector runs and offered beside every other provider.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ip-milestone ip-milestone-wide">
        <span class="ip-stage">Planned</span>
        <h3>Vector Cloud, the software factory</h3>
        <p>Teams are not leaving Vercel and Supabase, so Vector Cloud works inside the accounts they already have instead of replacing them. Today Vector's agents publish to Vercel and run Supabase migrations there. Next, Vector Cloud is planned to run the whole line in the cloud, with no laptop open:</p>
        <ol class="ip-line">
          <li><b>Intake</b><span>A GitHub issue, a ticket or a schedule starts the work.</span></li>
          <li><b>Build</b><span>Agents from any vendor, on any model, each in its own cloud copy of the repository.</span></li>
          <li><b>Check</b><span>The project's tests, the repair loop, a secret scan, Vectorscope review and a spend cap on every change.</span></li>
          <li><b>Preview</b><span>A pull request with a Vercel preview and its own Supabase branch database.</span></li>
          <li><b>Approve</b><span>A person merges and ships to production. The line never does that on its own.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <p>The line itself would live in the repository, so a team reviews and versions it like code and sees what each run cost. Several stations already run on the desktop: scheduled agents with spend caps, issue comments that become pull requests, Vectorscope review, and Vercel and Supabase controls.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="ip-footnote"><strong>Also on the way:</strong> the Run Guard in the terminal, with the next CLI release (desktop 1.99.108 already holds interactive runs to a live spend cap once you set one, and scheduled agents already stop at $5 a run and $25 a day by default), signed installers, and one-click sign-in for Vercel and Supabase (waiting on app registration).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ip-split" aria-label="Business model and limitations">
    <div class="ip-panel">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Business model</div>
      <h2>Free workspace, paid model credits</h2>
      <p class="ip-muted">The workspace stays free, which puts the agents, review and testing in front of every developer. Revenue is planned to come from <strong class="ip-ink">Vector Codium</strong>, a managed service for coding models: one account, no provider keys, the same curated models at every price. <span class="ip-status">In development</span></p>
      <div class="ip-tiers ip-num" aria-label="Plans">
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$10</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$50</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$100</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
        <span class="ip-tier"><b>$200</b> <span>/ mo</span></span>
      </div>
      <p class="ip-muted ip-small">Monthly plans or one-time top-ups of model credits, with no automatic overages. Teams that bring their own keys keep paying their provider directly.</p>
      <ol class="ip-flywheel">
        <li><b>Free workspace</b><span>Agents, review, testing and cost tracking bring developers in.</span></li>
        <li><b>Codium</b><span>Teams that want one bill and no keys buy model credits.</span></li>
        <li><b>Velocity</b><span>Once built, Codium would serve Vector's own model beside other labs', so more of that spend stays with Vector.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="ip-panel">
      <div class="ip-eyebrow">Limitations today</div>
      <h2>What is not there yet</h2>
      <ul class="ip-clean ip-limits">
        <li><strong>Very early.</strong> The first desktop previews shipped in July 2026, the app became free on 4 October 2026, and daily usage counts started on 10 October 2026, so there is no retention data yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Codex still wins on cost, speed and memory.</strong> In Vector's own matched live test (October 2026), Codex used less time, cost and memory per task. Desktop 1.99.108 cuts what Vector sends per request; the terminal's vector run, which the matched test used, gets the cuts with the next CLI release, and no matched live re-run has been done yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Claude Code is faster and lighter offline.</strong> In the archived later runs of the offline harness, on the integrated code they measured, Claude Code finished each scripted task in 0.4–1.0 seconds against Vector's 3.0–3.8, with about half the peak memory (326–350 MiB against 656–804); Vector's baseline there was slower still. Vector ran from source there, the others as installed builds.</li>
        <li><strong>Velocity is a plan, not a model yet:</strong> no weights and no benchmarks.</li>
        <li><strong>Vectorscope reviews GitHub only,</strong> with no GitLab or Bitbucket review yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Unsigned installers.</strong> macOS and Windows show a warning on first launch until code signing is set up.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows and Linux trail macOS</strong> in testing and maturity.</li>
        <li><strong>No SOC 2, SSO or admin console</strong> for larger companies yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Interactive spend caps are opt-in.</strong> Desktop 1.99.108 holds interactive sessions and parallel agents to a live spend cap only once you set one, in Settings, Spend limits or a project's budget.run; none is set by default, and only scheduled agents stop at a default cap. In the terminal, the vector run --max-cost and --max-steps flags arrive with the next CLI release, and Windows WSL servers keep the 1.99.99 engine, without the cap, until a later desktop release requires the new CLI. Vercel and Supabase connect with pasted tokens until hosted sign-in is approved.</li>
        <li><strong>Built on open source.</strong> The agent core is adapted from an MIT-licensed open-source coding agent; the workspace, orchestration, cost and review layers are Vector's own.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

